  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA positively tilted H5 trof will move across the Great Lakes on\nWednesday night into Thursday as a sfc low develops near the OH\nValley and then swings offshore of the Mid-Atlantic coast \nThursday night. This low is then progged to move northeastward \ntowards Cape Cod into Friday before exiting near Newfoundland by\nlate Saturday. A fairly large spread in guidance continues this\nevening in terms of the eventual track of this low and the \nseverity of any possible impacts across the local region.\n\nLatest 18Z guidance has trended upward a bit on QPF, with latest\nprobabilities for >=0.50\" now generally between 50-70%, highest\nsouth of the mtns. This is due to a slightly more westward track\nalong with a longer residence time of precipitation, which could\nbegin as early as Thursday afternoon and then persist in waves\nthrough part of Saturday. It is too early to determine if this\ntrend will persist as we get closer in time or if guidance will\ntrend drier once again. In terms of precipitation type, while \nsnow accumulations are less likely south of the foothills, some \nfestive flakes cannot be completely ruled out all the way to the\ncoast along with southern portions of the forecast area. The \nhighest potential for some wintry driving conditions continues \nto be favored from the foothills and points north, especially \nduring the nighttime and early morning hours when solar \ninsolation is not a factor. We will continue to monitor this\nsystem over the coming days as forecast details become clearer.\n\nOnce the coastal storm pulls away, high pressure builds down \ninto the CONUS from the Canadian Prairies. There will be some \nmoderation of temps over the Great Lakes, but overall it looks a\nfavorable set up for temps to average below normal. Daytime \nhighs should be near normal, but ideal radiational cooling early\nnext week should allow overnight readings to fall well below \nnormal.",
